      "<strong>Why the gap is so large.</strong> Japanese agency prices reflect Japanese costs: office rent in Tokyo and Osaka, salaries for directors, designers, engineers and sales staff, and in the wider IT industry a multi-layer subcontracting structure where a prime contractor passes work to second and third-tier companies, each adding a margin. Those costs are real, and some projects justify them, especially when you need in-person workshops or a vendor who writes Japanese copy. Our costs are lower because we are three engineers working remotely from India, without office, sales or subcontracting layers. That is the honest reason our starting prices are lower, not a difference in the care we put into the work.",
      `<strong>The exchange rate.</strong> We price in US dollars. The yen has been weak against the dollar, moving between roughly ¥153 and ¥157 per US dollar in September 2026, which makes any dollar price more expensive in yen than it was a few years ago. Even so, our starting prices sit well below the Japanese ranges above. If you prefer certainty in yen, we fix a JPY amount in the written quote.`,
      `<strong>Maintenance is the closest comparison.</strong> Server-only plans in Japan can cost as little as ¥5,000 a month, but they do not include content updates or fixes. Our plan from ${both("Maintenance")} includes content updates, bug fixes, security updates, backups and speed checks, which Japanese agencies typically price at ¥20,000 to ¥50,000 a month, and the first five months after launch are free.`,
