  {
    id: "japanese-websites",
    heading: "Japanese-language websites: typography, forms and the details that matter",
    paragraphs: [
      "A Japanese website is not an English website with the words swapped. Fonts, line breaking, forms, names and addresses all work differently, and Japanese visitors notice immediately when a site was built without understanding them. This is where an engineering team can add real value even when the words themselves come from a native writer.",
      "<strong>Fonts and performance.</strong> Japanese fonts contain thousands of glyphs, so a full web font such as Noto Sans JP can weigh several megabytes and slow every page. We either use the system fonts Japanese devices already have, such as Hiragino on Apple devices and Yu Gothic or Meiryo on Windows, or load subset web fonts that contain only the characters the site uses. Japanese text also needs more generous line height than English, usually around 1.7 to 2.0, and should never use fake italics, because Japanese fonts do not have italic styles.",
      `<strong>Line breaking.</strong> Japanese has no spaces between words, so browsers can break lines in awkward places, leaving a single character on a line or splitting a word in a heading. We apply strict line-breaking rules (${ja("禁則処理")}) so punctuation never starts a line, use phrase-aware breaking in headings where browsers support it, and check every key page on real phones. Vertical writing can be used for design accents, but body text stays horizontal for readability on screens.`,
      `<strong>Forms.</strong> Japanese forms follow conventions that foreign templates break. Names are entered family name first, usually in separate fields, and a furigana field (${ja("フリガナ")}) records the reading so staff can pronounce and sort names. A seven-digit postal code should fill in the prefecture, city and district automatically, and addresses run from prefecture down to building and room number. Many people type full-width numbers such as ０３, so we normalise full-width and half-width characters rather than rejecting the input. A confirmation screen (${ja("確認画面")}) before submission is expected on many business sites, and required fields are marked clearly as ${ja("必須")}.`,
      "<strong>Dates, prices and numbers.</strong> Dates are written year, month, day, and some audiences, especially in administrative or older-customer contexts, expect the Japanese era year such as Reiwa alongside the Western year. Consumer prices must be shown including consumption tax. Large numbers are often read in units of ten thousand (man), so price tables are easier to scan when they follow the way Japanese buyers read amounts.",
      "<strong>URLs and structure.</strong> Japanese characters in URLs are percent-encoded when shared, which turns a short address into a long string of symbols. We usually recommend readable English or romaji URLs with Japanese page titles and headings, which works well for search and sharing. For bilingual sites, each language has its own URL path, such as /ja/ and /en/, with hreflang tags linking equivalent pages.",
      "<strong>Translation and tone.</strong> Machine translation into Japanese is readable but rarely natural, and business Japanese uses levels of politeness that tools often get wrong. We keep all site text in a structured sheet so your translator or bilingual staff can work on it directly, then import it without copy-paste errors. We check that every page still fits once the Japanese text is in place, because Japanese and English text have very different lengths and shapes.",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "ecommerce-japan",
    heading: "Ecommerce in Japan: konbini, JCB, PayPay, Shopify and delivery",
    paragraphs: [
      "Japan is one of the world's largest ecommerce markets. According to the Ministry of Economy, Trade and Industry, consumer ecommerce reached ¥26.1 trillion in 2024, up 5.1 percent, while the online share of goods sales was still under 10 percent. Amazon.co.jp, Rakuten Ichiba and Yahoo! Shopping dominate, but more brands now run their own store alongside the marketplaces to keep customer relationships and margins.",
      "<strong>Payments.</strong> Japanese shoppers use a wider mix of payment methods than most markets. Credit cards are essential, and that must include JCB, Japan's own card brand, alongside Visa, Mastercard, American Express and Diners. Konbini payment lets customers pay cash at convenience stores such as FamilyMart and Lawson using a payment number. Bank transfer (furikomi) is standard for B2B orders. Cash on delivery is still used, code payments such as PayPay, d Barai, au PAY and Rakuten Pay are everyday habits, Amazon Pay reduces form filling, and deferred payment services such as NP Atobarai and Paidy let customers pay after the goods arrive. Stripe supports cards, konbini and bank transfer for Japanese businesses; Japanese providers such as KOMOJU, GMO Payment Gateway, SB Payment Service and PAY.JP cover further methods. Japanese payment providers usually require a Japanese company and bank account and review your store before approval, so every merchant account is opened in your company's name, never ours.",
      "<strong>Platform choice.</strong> Shopify suits most small and mid-sized stores: hosting, security, payments and upgrades are handled for you, the admin is in Japanese, and there are apps for delivery date selection, noshi wrapping and Japanese payment methods. WooCommerce suits businesses already on WordPress that want lower platform costs and full control of their data. A custom headless build suits unusual cases such as B2B price lists per customer, subscription food boxes with complex rules, or catalogues that sync with an existing core system. If you already use a Japanese cart such as BASE, STORES, MakeShop, futureshop or EC-CUBE, we will tell you honestly whether moving is worth it.",
      "<strong>Delivery.</strong> Japanese customers expect to choose a delivery date and time slot, and they expect it to be met. We configure Yamato, Sagawa or Japan Post shipping, refrigerated and frozen delivery for food, leave-at-door options and tracking emails. Since overtime limits for truck drivers took effect in April 2024, shipping costs and delivery windows have changed across the industry, so free-shipping thresholds and delivery promises deserve a fresh look.",
      "<strong>Gifts and seasons.</strong> Gift giving drives a large share of Japanese ecommerce, especially the summer ochugen and year-end oseibo seasons. Gift stores need noshi paper options with the correct inscription, gift messages, delivery to a different address, packing slips without prices and bulk orders to many recipients. Seasonal landing pages and early-order campaigns matter more here than discount codes.",
      "<strong>Prices, receipts and tax.</strong> Prices shown to consumers must include consumption tax, and food products generally use the reduced 8 percent rate while most other goods use 10 percent. Business customers often ask for a downloadable receipt (ryoshusho) addressed to their company name, which is easy to offer and reduces support emails. Every Japanese store also needs a page under the Specified Commercial Transactions Act, covered in the compliance section below.",
      "<strong>Selling abroad from Japan.</strong> Japanese products, from sake and tea to knives, ceramics and cosmetics, have strong demand overseas. Cross-border stores need English and often Chinese pages, multi-currency display, international shipping rates and clear information about import rules in each destination. We can run a cross-border store alongside your Japanese store with shared inventory.",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "seo-japan",
    heading: "SEO in Japan: Google, Yahoo! JAPAN, MEO and Japanese keywords",
    paragraphs: [
      "Google is the dominant search engine in Japan, and Yahoo! JAPAN, which many people still use, runs on Google's search technology. Good Google SEO therefore covers both. Bing is used on many Windows desktops through Edge, and we make sure sites are indexed there too, since it also powers several AI assistants.",
      "<strong>Technical foundations.</strong> Every site we build ships with clean URLs, unique titles and descriptions, structured data for your organisation, services, products and FAQs, an XML sitemap, a sensible robots.txt, correct canonical tags and redirects from any old URLs. We connect Google Search Console and check that pages are actually indexed, not just published. For redesigns we map every URL that currently receives traffic and redirect it properly, because most ranking losses after a redesign come from broken URLs rather than design.",
      `<strong>Japanese keyword research.</strong> The same idea can be written in kanji, hiragana, katakana, English and romaji, and each version has a different search volume. People looking for a website might search ${ja("ホームページ制作")}, ${ja("HP制作")}, ${ja("ホームページ作成")} or ${ja("Web制作")}. Japanese searchers also add words such as ${ja("費用")} or ${ja("料金")} (cost), ${ja("相場")} (market rate), ${ja("比較")} (comparison), ${ja("口コミ")} (reviews) and ${ja("おすすめ")} (recommended). We combine tool data with your Search Console queries and the questions your staff hear every day, and a native writer turns the plan into Japanese pages.`,
      `<strong>Local search and MEO.</strong> Japanese marketers call local search optimisation MEO, map engine optimisation. Your Google Business Profile must be owned and verified by you at your real address; we guide you on categories, services, photos and posts, and we never create profiles at addresses you do not operate from. Portals such as ${ja("食べログ")} for restaurants, ${ja("ホットペッパービューティー")} for salons, SUUMO for real estate and ${ja("じゃらん")} for travel rank for many local searches, so your own site should work alongside those listings and give customers a reason to book directly without commission.`,
      `<strong>Trust and expertise.</strong> For medical, legal, financial and other high-trust topics, Japanese search results favour clearly authored content. A common Japanese practice is to show that a qualified professional has supervised the content (${ja("監修")}), with their name, qualification and profile. We build author and supervisor profiles into the site structure and mark them up with structured data. We do not write claims about results, and regulated industries have extra advertising rules described in the compliance section.`,
      "<strong>International search.</strong> For inbound tourism and exporters, we build separate language versions with hreflang tags for Japanese, English, Simplified Chinese, Traditional Chinese and Korean, so each audience lands on the right page.",
      `<strong>Reporting.</strong> Monthly SEO starts from ${both("Monthly SEO")}, against a typical ¥100,000 to ¥300,000 a month for small businesses in Japan. Each month you get a short report from Search Console and analytics showing what changed, what we did and what we plan next. We do not guarantee rankings, because nobody controls Google's results.`,
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "inbound",
    heading: "Websites for inbound tourism and overseas buyers",
    paragraphs: [
      "Inbound tourism is one of Japan's fastest-growing industries. The Japan National Tourism Organization reported a record 42.68 million international visitors in 2025, the first year above 40 million, up from 36.87 million in 2024. South Korea, mainland China and Taiwan were the largest markets. Those visitors research on their phones, in their own languages, long before they arrive.",
      "<strong>What inbound sites need.</strong> Hotels, ryokan, restaurants, tour operators, ski schools and experience providers need clear pages in English, Simplified Chinese, Traditional Chinese for Taiwan and Hong Kong, and Korean. Prices in yen, online booking that accepts overseas cards, access directions from the nearest station or airport, house rules, cancellation policies and allergy or dietary information all reduce messages and no-shows. Mainland Chinese visitors often plan on platforms such as Xiaohongshu rather than Google, and Korean visitors use Naver alongside Google, so your site should be easy to link to from those channels.",
      "<strong>Direct bookings.</strong> Online travel agencies such as Booking.com, Expedia, Agoda, Trip.com and Airbnb bring guests but charge commission. A fast, multilingual site with a clear booking button is how accommodation and activity businesses win repeat and direct bookings. We connect your existing booking engine or channel manager rather than rebuilding one, and add structured data so search engines understand your prices, location and reviews.",
      "<strong>Tax-free shopping.</strong> Retailers that sell to visitors should keep an eye on the tax-free system, which Japan is changing to a refund model from November 2026. Product and store pages that explain tax-free purchases need to reflect the rules that apply at the time of purchase.",
      "<strong>Overseas buyers for Japanese manufacturers.</strong> Many Japanese manufacturers and producers have excellent products but a Japanese-only website. Overseas procurement teams need English specifications, certifications, minimum order quantities, lead times and a way to request a quotation. An English B2B site with a searchable catalogue and a clear quotation form often produces enquiries from abroad within months, and we build it so your Japanese staff can update products without touching code.",
      "<strong>Translation quality.</strong> We build the multilingual structure, but the Chinese, Korean and English text should be written or reviewed by native translators. Poor translation damages trust with exactly the visitors you want to attract.",
    ],
  },
  {
    id: "line-automation",
    heading: "LINE, kintone, freee and AI automation for Japanese teams",
    paragraphs: [
      "Japan's working-age population is shrinking, and many businesses cannot hire enough staff at any salary. Automating repetitive admin, such as retyping orders, chasing booking confirmations and copying data between tools, is often the fastest return on a small technology budget, and it is where our AI and data background is most useful.",
      `<strong>LINE.</strong> LINE is Japan's everyday messaging app, with more than 100 million monthly users in Japan according to LY Corporation. A LINE Official Account (${ja("LINE公式アカウント")}) lets businesses reach customers where they already are. With the Messaging API we can send booking confirmations and reminders for salons, clinics and restaurants, follow up on enquiries, build rich menus, link LINE Login to member accounts and create small LIFF apps, such as membership and stamp cards, that open inside LINE. Message volume affects the plan you pay LINE for, so we design flows that send useful messages rather than many. Since LINE Notify ended in March 2025, internal notifications now go through the Messaging API, Slack, Chatwork or Microsoft Teams.`,
      "<strong>Connecting the tools Japanese teams use.</strong> Many Japanese SMEs run on kintone for business data, Chatwork or Slack for internal chat, freee or Money Forward Cloud for accounting, and Google Workspace or Microsoft 365. We connect them so a website enquiry creates a kintone record and a Chatwork alert, a paid Shopify order creates an invoice in freee, and a new customer is added to the right LINE segment.",
      "<strong>FAX, PDF and paper.</strong> Many B2B orders in Japan still arrive by FAX or as PDF attachments. AI-OCR can read those orders, extract product codes, quantities and delivery dates into kintone or a spreadsheet, and flag anything uncertain for a person to check. Since the Electronic Books Maintenance Act requires electronic transaction records to be stored electronically from January 2024, automations can also file invoices with searchable date, amount and counterparty fields.",
      "<strong>AI assistants, used carefully.</strong> Current language models handle Japanese well, including polite business forms, but tone still needs checking. We use AI to draft replies to common questions from your own price lists and policies, summarise long email threads and classify incoming requests, and we keep a person approving anything that commits the business, such as a quotation or a refund. Personal data access is limited to what each step needs.",
      `<strong>What it costs.</strong> AI automation starts from ${both("AI automation")} for one well-defined workflow, compared with ¥200,000 to ¥1,000,000 typical in Japan. Running costs such as LINE plans, API usage and automation platform subscriptions are paid directly by you to those providers, and we estimate them in the quote.`,
    ],
  },
